    correctAnswer: "A",
    explanation: "This patient has severe COPD (emphysema-predominant) based on the low FEV1/FVC ratio (<0.70), significantly reduced FEV1, and hyperinflation on chest x-ray.\n\nPhysical examination findings in emphysema:\n• Decreased tactile fremitus (air trapping reduces sound transmission)\n• Hyperresonance to percussion (increased air in lungs)\n• Decreased breath sounds\n• Barrel chest (increased AP diameter)\n• Prolonged expiratory phase\n• Use of accessory muscles\n\nDullness to percussion, egophony, and increased vocal resonance are seen with consolidation (pneumonia). Inspiratory crackles suggest interstitial lung disease or heart failure.",
    educationalObjective: "Emphysema causes air trapping that leads to decreased tactile fremitus, hyperresonance to percussion, and decreased breath sounds. These findings contrast with consolidation, which causes increased fremitus and dullness.",
