    correctAnswer: "B",
    explanation: "This patient has metabolic alkalosis (pH 7.48, HCO3 32 mEq/L) caused by thiazide diuretic use, with appropriate respiratory compensation.\n\nThiazide diuretics cause metabolic alkalosis through:\n• Volume contraction → increased proximal HCO3 reabsorption\n• Hypokalemia → intracellular K/H exchange, increased H+ secretion\n• Increased aldosterone → enhanced H+ secretion in collecting duct\n\nRespiratory compensation:\n• Hypoventilation to retain CO2\n• Expected PaCO2 rise: 0.7 × (HCO3 - 24)\n• For HCO3 of 32: Expected PaCO2 ≈ 40 + 0.7(8) = 45-46 mm Hg\n\nHypokalemia symptoms include muscle cramps, weakness, and fatigue.",
