    id: 50,
    stem: "A 3-year-old boy is brought to the physician because of a 1-week history of bruising easily and petechiae. He had a viral upper respiratory infection 2 weeks ago. Physical examination shows scattered petechiae on the trunk and extremities. There is no hepatosplenomegaly or lymphadenopathy. Laboratory studies show hemoglobin of 12.5 g/dL, white blood cell count of 8,000/mm³ with normal differential, and platelet count of 15,000/mm³. Peripheral blood smear shows decreased platelets that are normal to large in size. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options: [
      { label: "A", text: "Acute lymphoblastic leukemia" },
      { label: "B", text: "Hemolytic uremic syndrome" },
      { label: "C", text: "Henoch-Schönlein purpura" },
      { label: "D", text: "Immune thrombocytopenic purpura" },
      { label: "E", text: "Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura" },
    ],
    correctAnswer: "D",
    explanation: "This child has immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP), characterized by isolated thrombocytopenia following a viral infection in an otherwise healthy child.\n\nITP features:\n• Isolated thrombocytopenia (often <20,000/mm³)\n• Otherwise normal CBC\n• Large platelets on smear (compensatory)\n• Preceding viral illness (1-4 weeks prior)\n• No hepatosplenomegaly or lymphadenopathy\n• Mucocutaneous bleeding (petechiae, bruising)\n\nPathophysiology:\n• Antiplatelet antibodies (usually IgG) target platelet antigens\n• Antibody-coated platelets destroyed by splenic macrophages\n• Cross-reactivity with viral antigens may trigger antibody formation\n\nMost cases in children resolve spontaneously within 6 months. Treatment (if symptomatic or very low platelets): IVIG, corticosteroids, or anti-D immunoglobulin.",
    educationalObjective: "ITP in children presents with isolated thrombocytopenia following viral illness. It is characterized by antiplatelet antibodies and typically resolves spontaneously.",
